.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt PAM_CAPABILITY 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pam_capability
.Nd establish a policy-scoped process discovery context
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
session module uses the login provider's existing discovery authority to
request a session from BSDAuth for the PAM account.
BSDAuth selects grants from principal policy; the module does not infer
authority from UID 0, group membership, or an environment variable.
The module replaces inherited provider authority before user code runs.
A failed mint leaves discovery absent while ordinary UNIX login continues.
Failure to clear inherited authority returns a PAM session error.
.Pp
The context is held by the kernel and survives fork, exec, descriptor cleanup,
and environment replacement.
It is bound to the target real UID and prison.
The provider must complete its normal credential transition before launching
the session.
The module does not authenticate a password or change UNIX IDs.
.Pp
Place this module in the session stack of trusted login providers, such as
providers including the base login stack.
Do not add it a second time to providers that already explicitly provision
sessions, such as the current su and SSH adapters.
.Pp
Closing a PAM session does not revoke contexts inherited by other processes.
Process exit releases its held reference.
Already issued capabilities retain
their own lifetime and revocation semantics.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width unprivileged
.It Cm unprivileged
Request USER discovery without the principal's anointments or administrative
rights.
The base cron and atrun session stacks use this option for unattended jobs.
It narrows a mint and never grants authentication authority to the caller.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr pam.conf 5
